$DIR/main.p:8:7: stats uses the per-CPU variable stats outside a region where the task cannot move to another processor
    stats has one copy for each processor. This is an access to a variable
    larger than a machine word, so the task could move to another processor in
    the middle of it and read one processor's copy and write another's. It must
    be inside a region where the task cannot move: preemption disabled
    (rt.DisablePreemption to rt.EnablePreemption), maskable interrupts disabled,
    or an rt.Lock held. A lone load or store of a whole variable of at most a
    machine word is allowed anywhere.
    Go has no per-processor variables. A Plutonium kernel keeps its processor's
    state in them, and an ordinary variable would be shared by every processor
    and need a lock. The compiler checks the uses instead, so that the most
    common use (a pointer to the running thread) stays safe code.
    Put the access inside a region where the task cannot move:
        rt.DisablePreemption()
        defer rt.EnablePreemption()
        // the access to stats
    (see "Per-CPU variables" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2022" for a longer explanation)
